    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ee0e3466-d9a6-5c68-87d0-aa9d20a2d700",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $9 \\sqrt{7}\\mathbf{a} + 21\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\lambda (3\\mathbf{a} + \\sqrt{7}\\mathbf{b})$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $\\lambda .$\n\n$\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Since the vectors are equal, we can write\n\n$$\n9 \\sqrt{7}\\mathbf{a} + 21\\mathbf{b}=\\lambda (3\\mathbf{a} + \\sqrt{7}\\mathbf{b}).\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the left-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n3 \\sqrt{7}(3\\mathbf{a} + \\sqrt{7}\\mathbf{b})=\\lambda (3\\mathbf{a} + \\sqrt{7}\\mathbf{b}).\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $(3\\mathbf{a} + \\sqrt{7}\\mathbf{b})$ on both sides. So, let's define a new vector $u$ as\n\n$$\nu=3\\mathbf{a} + \\sqrt{7}\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nThen, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n3 \\sqrt{7}\\,u=\\lambda u.\n$$\n\nSince $u$ is non-zero,\n\n$$\n\\lambda=3 \\sqrt{7}.\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "ca9a188b-9ce3-56d9-9255-b0f37d553e2c",
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
